WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.500
A somewhat controversial legacy and&nbsp;
a lifetime spent in the public eye.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.860
Jerry Springer might be gone,&nbsp;
but he won't soon be forgotten.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:13.680
Jerry Springer was a TV icon for decades, best&nbsp;
known for his self-titled talk show that kicked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.020
off in 1991. Springer himself would admit the&nbsp;
show changed pop culture for better and worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.080
ushering in other shows of its&nbsp;
ilk that could affectionately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.520
be referred to as "trash TV." The&nbsp;
show was synonymous with the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.900
but some may be surprised to learn it&nbsp;
aired new episodes all the way until 2018.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.600
But even when the show ended, Springer didn't&nbsp;
leave television. He had another show lined up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.600
called "Judge Jerry," where he took on legal cases&nbsp;
and ran for three seasons. In March 2022, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.940
TV personality spoke about how his other series&nbsp;
just didn't hit the same way as "Jerry Springer,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.460
and COVID-19 and its social distancing policies&nbsp;
didn't help matters. He told The New York Post,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.120
"I was talking to parties that were&nbsp;
thousands of miles away — wherever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.600
the suits were filed was where we put&nbsp;
the cameras — and you've got that delay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.160
no audience and [me] sitting in front of a&nbsp;
green screen. It wasn't what I first imagined."

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.900
But there was another issue Springer found himself&nbsp;
up against trying to make "Judge Jerry" work.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.740
With so many episodes of "Jerry&nbsp;
Springer" produced over the decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.600
it isn't hard to still catch an episode if you&nbsp;
have cable. Reruns still air on syndication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.180
which likely netted Springer a nice paycheck&nbsp;
of residuals every so often but also hampered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.920
his efforts to expand into other series. COVID&nbsp;
played a role in the demise of "Judge Jerry,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:33.360
but he was also his own worst enemy, as&nbsp;
he went on to tell The New York Post,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.500
"In most markets they still run 'The Jerry&nbsp;
Springer Show,' that's been holding on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.660
and there's only so much of me that a normal&nbsp;
human being can take. It was a containment issue;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.000
if I was going to be so crazy on that show,&nbsp;
how can I suddenly be serious on the bench?"

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.980
"Okay, I'm putting like health, happiness, peace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.260
and prosperity in the room&nbsp;
so you can all feel that."

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.860
There's only so much Jerry Springer one&nbsp;
can take, and when it came time to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.840
"Judge Jerry," Springer fully understood&nbsp;
why it had to happen. He continued to say,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.420
"I enjoyed doing ['Judge Jerry'], no question,&nbsp;
but the industry isn't based on, 'Gee, are you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.400
having fun doing it?' It wasn't making the money&nbsp;
it was making in the beginning. I totally get it."

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.640
The success of one show doesn't&nbsp;
necessarily translate to another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.840
so in 2022, the show was&nbsp;
canceled after three seasons.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:23.760
When The New York Post interview was&nbsp;
conducted in 2022, Jerry Springer was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.720
obviously looking back on his life and&nbsp;
figuring out what it all meant. You could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.680
spend a long time debating the merits of his&nbsp;
show and what it did for the culture at large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.420
but for Jerry, his hit series wasn't even&nbsp;
his biggest accomplishment. He elaborated,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.640
"The greatest job I ever had was being mayor&nbsp;
of Cincinnati, and if God came to me and said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.020
'You can do one thing and you can do it for&nbsp;
your lifetime,' I would have chosen that."

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.420
Legacy is a complex question, especially when&nbsp;
it comes to public figures. Even Springer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.740
admitted that most people aren't remembered&nbsp;
a couple of decades after they pass away, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.640
that really wasn't what he was angling for.&nbsp;
He just wanted to be there for his family:

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.380
"The important thing is to be remembered by&nbsp;
your kids and your grandkids. If they love me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.280
and remember me and I've been a good role&nbsp;
model for them, that's all that matters."

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.120
Even though "Judge Jerry" ended sooner&nbsp;
than Springer probably would've liked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.880
his legacy in the realm of television is&nbsp;
secure. Jerry Springer died in April 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.240
but as he put it himself, his legacy will remain:

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.460
"As long as the crazy show keeps going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:26.580
I'll be a part of the fabric of&nbsp;
the memories of our culture."

